<p>
Here are the options for a built in automatic saving, so maybe I don't have to focus on any keybindings at all, but can I get used to it?
</p>

<p>
<code>auto-save-mode</code> is on by default in file-visiting buffers. It writes <code>#file#</code> beside the real file (TRAMP copies go to <code>/tmp</code>) after either 300 input events or 30 seconds of idle time. The original is untouched. It runs <code>auto-save-hook</code> but not <code>before-save-hook</code> or <code>after-save-hook</code>, so no formatter, VC refresh, or file watcher fires. Saving normally deletes <code>#file#</code>; after a crash, <code>M-x recover-file</code> or <code>M-x recover-session</code> gets it back.  It is the only one of the two that protects a long continuous typing burst.
</p>

<p>
<code>auto-save-visited-mode</code> (Emacs 26.1+) is global and off by default. After five seconds of idle it calls <code>save-some-buffers</code> with no prompt and writes the real files. It runs the full save hooks, VC refreshes, backups, and watchers, and can create a file you were only testing. It also saves every modified file buffer, not just the current one, unless you narrow it with <code>auto-save-visited-predicate</code> (Emacs 29.1+), a buffer-local nil, or <code>remote-file-name-inhibit-auto-save-visited</code> for TRAMP.
</p>

<div class="org-src-container">
<pre class="src src-elisp"><code>(<span style="color: #569cd6; font-weight: bold;">setq</span> auto-save-visited-interval 5)
(<span style="color: #569cd6; font-weight: bold;">setq</span> remote-file-name-inhibit-auto-save-visited t)
(<span style="color: #569cd6; font-weight: bold;">setq</span> auto-save-visited-predicate
      (<span style="color: #569cd6; font-weight: bold;">lambda</span> ()
        (<span style="color: #569cd6; font-weight: bold;">and</span> (derived-mode-p 'org-mode)
             (not (file-remote-p buffer-file-name)))))
(auto-save-visited-mode 1)
</code></pre>
</div>

<p>
That is the version I am trying: Org only, no TRAMP. It needs Emacs 29.1+ for the predicate; older versions would save every modified file buffer.
</p>

<p>
I am keeping <code>auto-save-mode</code> at its defaults and adding visited mode narrowly. Then I will live with it and see: does five seconds feel right?, do the hooks and VC refreshes annoy me?, does it save something I meant to discard?, and do I stop thinking about the save key (is this something I really want?)? If yes, the experiment wins. If not, I will know which side effects actually matter. I will still save deliberately before a commit or a risky change. Auto-save is a safety net, not a save point. I will don't worry report back, probably craving for a keybinding, but you never know!
</p>
